'use strict';
const JourneyEngine=(()=>{
const escape=s=>String(s??'').replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
const norm=s=>String(s||'').toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,' ').trim();
const fields={title:'Daily title',scripture:'Scripture to read',scene:'Begin with Scripture',teaching:'Teaching & reflection',ponder:'A point to ponder',practice:'Practice today',questions:'Three reflection questions',prayer:'Prayer',next:'My next step'};
// Original editorial reflections, not recovered source manuscripts or a reproduction of another book.
const beats=[
['Begin by listening','Luke 10:38–42','Mary sits at Jesus’ feet and listens while Martha is distracted by the work of hospitality. Jesus addresses Martha’s worry and draws attention to Mary’s choice.','A faithful beginning may require attention before activity. Receiving Jesus’ teaching creates room to examine what has been driving us. Work and care still matter; the question is whether our busyness leaves room to listen.','Set aside ten unhurried minutes. Read the passage twice and write down what you notice.'],
['You are entrusted with a life','Genesis 1:26–31','The opening chapter of Genesis describes humanity as made in God’s image and given responsibility within creation. The account places human life within God’s good work.','Your worth precedes your productivity. Responsibility grows out of being a creature loved and entrusted by God, rather than an attempt to earn a place through accomplishment. Consider where gratitude could change the way you approach today’s responsibilities.','Name one person or responsibility you have been entrusted to care for.'],
['Take the next faithful step','Genesis 12:1–4','Abram receives God’s call to leave his country and go to a land God will show him. The account moves from God’s promise to Abram’s response.','Faith does not require pretending that uncertainty has disappeared. It involves responding to what God has made clear. Abram’s specific calling is his own; our response begins with the responsibilities and teaching God has already placed before us.','Write one clear responsibility you can act on without knowing every future outcome.'],
['Notice what you carry','Matthew 11:28–30','Jesus invites the weary to come to him, take his yoke, and learn from him. His invitation brings rest together with discipleship.','Rest in Christ is more than a brief escape from responsibility. Learning his gentleness reshapes how we carry it. We can name our fatigue honestly and ask which burdens belong to our calling and which arise from expectations we have never examined.','List what feels heavy. Ask a trusted person to help you identify one burden you can share.'],
['Ask for wisdom','James 1:2–8','James addresses believers facing trials and tells those who lack wisdom to ask God. The invitation is grounded in God’s generosity.','Wisdom helps us respond faithfully when circumstances do not become easy. Asking for it is an admission that our first reaction may not be our best guide. Prayer can open us to correction, patience, and counsel as we choose what to do.','Bring one unresolved decision to prayer. Write the question before proposing an answer.'],
['Make room for honest prayer','Psalm 13','The psalmist asks how long suffering will continue, petitions God for help, and turns toward trust in God’s steadfast love. The prayer includes both distress and hope.','Honesty belongs in prayer. We do not need to make pain sound tidy before bringing it to God. This psalm offers language for staying in relationship with God while circumstances remain unresolved. Trust can coexist with a question we cannot yet answer.','Write a short prayer that includes what hurts, what you need, and what you are trusting God with.'],
['Remember before moving on','Deuteronomy 8:1–10','Moses calls Israel to remember the wilderness and God’s provision as they prepare for life in the land. Remembering is part of faithful living.','It is easy to hurry past gifts once they become familiar. Remembering helps us resist the illusion that we have sustained ourselves alone. Look back with gratitude while recognizing the people and provision that helped you reach this day.','Record three gifts from the past week and thank someone who helped you.'],
['Love has a practical shape','Luke 10:25–37','Jesus tells of a wounded traveler and a Samaritan who stops, tends his wounds, and arranges continuing care. The question of neighbor becomes a call to mercy.','Love interrupts convenience. The Samaritan’s response includes attention, action, and follow-through. We cannot meet every need, but limits do not remove the invitation to respond to the person in front of us with concrete care.','Choose one act of practical care that fits your capacity today.'],
['Listen before you answer','James 1:19–25','James calls believers to be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to anger, then urges them to put the word into practice.','Listening is a form of humility. It gives another person room to be understood before our conclusions take over. Receiving Scripture also requires this posture: hearing should lead to an honest response, rather than becoming a substitute for obedience.','In one conversation, summarize what you heard before offering your own view.'],
['Welcome someone to the table','Romans 12:9–13','Paul describes sincere love through devotion, service, patient hope, prayer, generosity, and hospitality. These practices give community its everyday texture.','Belonging is built through repeated acts of welcome. A meal, a thoughtful question, or a remembered need can make an abstract commitment to love visible. Hospitality need not be impressive to be meaningful. It begins with making room.','Invite someone into a simple conversation or shared meal without pressure to reciprocate.'],
['Speak words that build','Ephesians 4:25–32','Paul connects the new life in Christ with truthful speech, responsible anger, generosity, and words that build others up.','Words can make truth easier or harder to receive. Building another person up does not require avoiding difficult subjects. It asks us to speak with honesty, timing, and a desire for their good, rather than using truth to secure a victory.','Rewrite one difficult statement so it is truthful, specific, and gracious.'],
['Practice a generous interpretation','Philippians 2:1–8','Paul urges believers toward humility and concern for others, directing their attention to the self-giving example of Christ.','Humility loosens the need to make ourselves the center of every situation. Considering another person’s interests does not mean ignoring harm or abandoning wise boundaries. It means approaching people as persons to love rather than obstacles to overcome.','Ask what someone else may need before deciding how to respond.'],
['Take responsibility for repair','Matthew 5:23–24','Jesus describes someone bringing a gift to the altar who remembers a fractured relationship. He gives reconciliation urgent attention.','Worship and relationships belong together. Repair begins with taking responsibility for our part rather than demanding that another person change first. Some situations require support and careful boundaries; reconciliation should never be used to pressure someone into danger.','Where it is appropriate and safe, acknowledge one specific wrong without adding an excuse.'],
['Bear a burden together','Galatians 6:1–5','Paul urges gentle restoration, attention to oneself, and mutual burden-bearing. The passage holds shared care alongside personal responsibility.','Community makes space for both help and agency. We can support another person without taking control of their decisions, and receive care without treating our own participation as unnecessary. Gentleness guides both giving and receiving.','Ask someone what kind of help would be useful instead of assuming.'],
['Stay connected to the source','John 15:1–11','Jesus uses the image of a vine and branches to describe abiding in him. Fruitfulness is connected to remaining in his love and teaching.','Lasting growth depends on relationship with Christ. Activity can look fruitful while leaving us disconnected from the one who gives life. Return to the practices that help you receive his teaching and respond in love. Growth may be quieter than you expected.','Choose a daily time to pause, read, and pray for the rest of this journey.'],
['Let the word take root','Mark 4:1–20','Jesus tells the parable of seed falling on different kinds of soil and explains how the word is received. Distraction and pressure affect whether it bears fruit.','The question is more than whether we have heard something true. It is how we make room for it. Notice which distractions crowd out reflection and which pressures lead you to set aside what you believe. Small changes can make attention more possible.','Remove one recurring distraction from your reading time.'],
['Renew a familiar pattern','Romans 12:1–2','Paul calls believers to offer their lives to God and describes transformation through the renewing of the mind. Discernment develops within this response.','Patterns shape us even when we do not notice them. Renewal begins by bringing a familiar assumption into the light of God’s mercy. Instead of demanding an instant reinvention, identify one way of thinking that needs to be tested and practiced differently.','Write a familiar assumption and examine it alongside today’s passage.'],
['Be faithful in a small thing','Luke 16:10–13','Jesus connects faithfulness in small matters with larger responsibilities and warns against divided allegiance between God and wealth.','Small choices reveal what we serve. Faithfulness does not wait for an impressive opportunity; it takes shape in ordinary commitments. Consider one quiet responsibility that deserves your attention even if no one else will applaud the effort.','Complete one small commitment you have been postponing.'],
['Choose an undivided yes','Matthew 6:19–24','Jesus teaches about treasure, the heart, and the impossibility of serving two masters. His words bring competing loyalties into view.','What we treasure directs our attention. This passage invites us to examine our attachments without reducing discipleship to a transaction. Ask what your choices reveal about the security, recognition, or control you have been pursuing.','Review one recent choice involving time or resources. Name the loyalty behind it.'],
['Receive before you give','1 John 4:7–12','John grounds Christian love in God’s prior love, made known in sending his Son. Loving one another flows from this gift.','We do not manufacture love by willpower alone. Receiving God’s love changes the source from which we offer care. When generosity becomes an attempt to prove our worth, return to the gift that came before any achievement.','Begin prayer with gratitude for God’s love before listing what you need to accomplish.'],
['Open your hands','2 Corinthians 9:6–8','Paul encourages willing generosity and describes God’s provision for good work. He distinguishes giving from reluctance or compulsion.','Generosity is a willing response, not a measure of someone’s worth or a promise of personal wealth. It can include money, attention, hospitality, and service. Consider what you can offer responsibly and freely, without comparison or pressure.','Choose a concrete gift that is appropriate to your circumstances.'],
['Count what already belongs to God','Psalm 24:1–6','The psalm declares that the earth and its fullness belong to the Lord, then asks who may stand in his holy place.','Stewardship begins with belonging to God. Possessions and abilities become responsibilities we hold for a time. This perspective does not solve every practical decision, but it changes the question from what we can claim to how we can care faithfully.','Name one resource you hold and one way it could serve another person’s good.'],
['Create space for enough','1 Timothy 6:6–10, 17–19','Paul warns against the love of money and calls those with resources to humility, hope in God, and generosity.','Contentment loosens the grip of comparison. It helps us distinguish a real need from an expanding appetite for more. We can plan responsibly while remembering that possessions cannot carry the weight of ultimate security.','Notice one comparison that fuels discontent and write a gratitude response.'],
['See the overlooked person','Mark 10:46–52','Bartimaeus calls out as Jesus passes. Though others tell him to be quiet, Jesus stops, calls him, and asks what he wants.','Attention can honor dignity. Jesus’ question gives Bartimaeus room to speak for himself. In our relationships, we can practice care that listens to a person’s expressed need rather than assuming we already know their story.','Ask an often-overlooked person a sincere question and make time for the answer.'],
['Use what you have received','1 Peter 4:8–11','Peter connects earnest love, hospitality, and service, calling believers to use their gifts as stewards of God’s grace.','Gifts become fruitful in service. You do not need someone else’s abilities to begin offering what you have received. Notice where your experience or capacity can meet an actual need, while giving others room to contribute their own gifts.','Offer one specific skill to someone who could use it.'],
['Let service set the direction','Mark 10:35–45','After James and John seek places of honor, Jesus teaches the disciples that greatness among them takes the shape of service.','Leadership is tested by whose good it seeks. Jesus redirects ambition toward service rather than status. Consider how a decision would change if the dignity and growth of the people affected became central to your definition of success.','Make one leadership choice that helps another person participate or grow.'],
['Pass on what matters','Deuteronomy 6:4–9','Israel is called to love the Lord and to speak of these words in ordinary daily life, including at home and along the way.','Formation happens through repeated conversation as well as formal teaching. Everyday moments can hold questions, stories, and practices that help faith become tangible. Passing on what matters starts with attending to how we live it ourselves.','Share one faith practice and invite a question rather than delivering a lecture.'],
['Tell the truth about your story','Psalm 78:1–7','The psalm calls the community to tell the coming generation about God’s deeds so that they may place their hope in God.','An honest story can become a gift. We can remember God’s faithfulness without editing out our limitations or claiming certainty about every event. Tell what you actually experienced and distinguish it from what you have come to understand.','Write a short true story of something you learned, including what remains unresolved.'],
['Offer encouragement with substance','1 Thessalonians 5:11–15','Paul calls believers to encourage and build one another up, care for people with differing needs, and pursue good together.','Encouragement becomes stronger when it is specific. Notice an effort, a choice, or a sign of growth. The passage also reminds us that people need different forms of care; patient attention helps us respond to the person rather than a formula.','Tell someone exactly what you appreciate about a recent action they took.'],
['Make room for the next person','2 Timothy 2:1–2','Paul tells Timothy to entrust what he has heard to faithful people who will be able to teach others also.','What has helped you grow may help someone else when it is shared responsibly. Passing it on requires more than handing over information. Make room for questions, practice, correction, and the other person’s own faithful response.','Invite someone to learn or practice one useful thing alongside you.'],
['Serve together','Ecclesiastes 4:9–12','The writer reflects on the value of companionship, shared work, help in difficulty, and mutual strength.','Partnership makes some forms of care more sustainable. Working together allows different strengths to contribute and creates opportunities to encourage one another. Consider where independence has become a habit that keeps you from receiving or offering help.','Plan one modest act of service with another person.'],
['Choose peace with integrity','Romans 12:14–21','Paul calls believers to bless rather than curse, seek peace as far as it depends on them, and overcome evil with good.','Peacemaking is active, but it does not give us control over another person’s response. We can choose truthful, non-retaliatory action while recognizing the limits of our responsibility. Seek wise support when conflict is complex or unsafe.','Identify the part of one conflict that is actually yours to address.'],
['Persevere without pretending','Hebrews 12:1–3','The writer calls believers to lay aside hindrances and run with endurance, looking to Jesus. The invitation includes remembering what he endured.','Endurance does not mean denying weariness. It means returning to the direction of faith with the help we need. Some burdens should be laid aside, and some responsibilities require patient continuation. Ask which is true of the situation before you.','Name one hindrance to release and one faithful practice to continue.'],
['Bring tomorrow into prayer','Philippians 4:4–9','Paul invites believers to bring requests to God with thanksgiving and to practice what they have learned. Prayer and action appear together.','Prayer creates space to name concern without allowing it to define every next step. This invitation does not dismiss anxiety or replace needed care. Bring a specific concern to God and consider one grounded action that is available today.','Write one concern, one thanksgiving, and one practical next action.'],
['Seek the good of your place','Jeremiah 29:4–7','Jeremiah’s letter tells the exiles to build lives where they are and seek the welfare of the city, praying on its behalf.','Faithfulness can include caring for a place we did not choose. The exiles’ situation is specific, but their call challenges the habit of postponing all responsibility until circumstances improve. Notice one good you can contribute where you actually are.','Pray for your neighborhood and choose one practical way to contribute.'],
['Measure what matters','Galatians 5:22–26','Paul describes the fruit of the Spirit and calls believers to keep in step with the Spirit rather than provoking or envying one another.','Growth includes the quality of our life with others. Numbers may help us plan, but they cannot by themselves reveal patience, gentleness, or faithfulness. Ask someone who knows you where they have noticed change, and listen without defending yourself.','Reflect on one relationship where patience or kindness is growing.'],
['Give thanks in community','Colossians 3:12–17','Paul describes compassion, kindness, humility, forgiveness, love, and gratitude as part of the community’s shared life in Christ.','Gratitude becomes fuller when it recognizes the people who have accompanied us. Giving thanks does not erase difficulty. It acknowledges grace within the actual story and invites us to carry that awareness into the next chapter.','Thank a person who has walked with you during this journey.'],
['Name what you will continue','James 2:14–18','James challenges a claim to faith that ignores a person’s practical need. His example connects professed belief with visible action.','A journey becomes meaningful when its learning continues in life. Choose a practice that is small enough to repeat and significant enough to serve someone’s good. Keep the commitment concrete so that you can return to it when enthusiasm changes.','Choose one practice, a time to do it, and a person who can encourage you.'],
['Make a next-step covenant','Joshua 24:14–24','Joshua calls Israel to choose whom they will serve and addresses the seriousness of their response. The people are invited into accountable commitment.','Commitment deserves more than a moment of intensity. State clearly what you intend to practice and what support you need. A next step is not a prediction that you will never struggle; it is an honest direction to which you can return.','Write a realistic commitment for the next thirty days and share it with someone you trust.'],
['Carry the journey outward','Matthew 28:16–20','The risen Jesus commissions his disciples to make disciples, teaching them to observe his commands, and promises his presence.','The ending of a reading plan can become the beginning of shared practice. What you have received is meant to shape how you love, serve, and accompany others. Continue with humility, remembering that Christ’s presence sustains the work.','Choose one person to encourage and schedule a conversation about your next season.']
];
const themes={formation:{name:'faith and spiritual growth',bridge:'Notice how receiving God’s grace can shape a response in your ordinary life. Formation involves attention, trust, and repeated practice, supported by a community where honest questions are welcome.',question:'Which part of my daily rhythm would help this truth take root?'},community:{name:'belonging and relationships',bridge:'Bring a real relationship to mind. Consider how the passage might shape your listening, welcome, boundaries, or willingness to repair. Shared growth begins when love takes a form another person can recognize.',question:'Who could experience greater welcome or care through my response?'},legacy:{name:'family and legacy',bridge:'Consider the relationships through which your values are becoming visible. A legacy is expressed in present choices as well as future plans. Make room for each person’s voice and avoid assuming everyone shares the same experience or pace.',question:'What could the next generation learn from the way I live this today?'},generosity:{name:'generosity and stewardship',bridge:'Reflect on the time, attention, abilities, and resources entrusted to you. A generous response can be thoughtful, freely chosen, and appropriate to your responsibilities. This is spiritual reflection, not a recommendation about a financial product or transaction.',question:'What can I offer freely and responsibly in response to this passage?'},leadership:{name:'purpose and leadership',bridge:'Think of the people affected by your decisions. Consider what serving their good would require in your present role. Purpose becomes concrete through choices that honor people, fulfill responsibilities, and leave room for wise counsel.',question:'How can I exercise responsibility in a way that helps someone else grow?'},peace:{name:'peace and resilience',bridge:'Attend to your actual capacity today. Faithful practice can include rest, support, and a small manageable action. Let this reflection accompany appropriate pastoral or professional care when your circumstances call for it.',question:'What small step toward support, rest, or wise action is available today?'}};
function restricted(r){return /^yes/i.test(r['Do Not Send to AI Yet?']||'')||/do not build|removed|reference only|market benchmark|park for later|deferred/i.test([r['Build Decision'],r['Source Item Type'],r.Notes].join(' '))||r['Priority Grade']==='REFERENCE';}
function records(d){const out=[],s=d.sections.find(s=>s.name==='Updated Master');for(const row of s?.rows||[]){const r=Object.fromEntries(s.columns.map((k,i)=>[k,row[i]??'']));out.push(convert(r,false));}for(const r of d.extraRecords||[])out.push(convert(r,true));for(const r of d.libraryUpdates?.journeys||[])out.push({id:'J-'+r.id,title:r.title,subtitle:r.subtitle||'',category:r.category||r.collection||'',audience:r.audience||'',source:r.collection||'Library updates',locator:r.id,blocked:false,reason:'Proposed concept; draft requires review',original:r});return out.filter(r=>String(r.title||'').trim());}
function convert(r,history){return{id:r['Master ID'],title:r['Campaign Title'],subtitle:r.Subtitle||'',category:r.Category||'',audience:r['Best For / Audience']||'',source:r['Source Document']||'',locator:r['Original Link / Reference']||'',blocked:history||restricted(r),reason:restricted(r)?'Source hold / reference restriction':history?'Historical or supplementary source; inspect before building':'Catalog concept; draft requires review',original:r};}
function build(p){const theme=themes[p.focus]||themes.formation,n=Number(p.duration);if(![21,30,40].includes(n))throw Error('Choose 21, 30, or 40 days.');if(!p.title?.trim())throw Error('Choose a title first.');const days=Array.from({length:n},(_,i)=>{const b=beats[Math.round(i*39/(n-1))];return{number:i+1,title:b[0],scripture:b[1],scene:b[2],teaching:b[3]+'\n\n'+theme.bridge+'\n\nAs you continue “'+p.title+'”, connect today’s passage to one concrete situation. '+(p.role==='Advisor'?'Use this as an invitation to conversation. Each person may share only what they choose.':'Bring your own questions and experiences into prayer and your next group conversation.'),ponder:b[0]+': one faithful response matters.',practice:b[4],questions:'1. What does this passage reveal about God and the people in its original setting?\n2. '+theme.question+'\n3. What will I do in response, and who can encourage me?',prayer:'God, help me receive your teaching with honesty. Give me wisdom to respond in love, courage to take a faithful step, and humility to receive help. Amen.',next:'My action:\nWhen I will take it:\nWho can encourage me:',reviewed:false};});return{schema:'LT-JOURNEY-1',created:new Date().toISOString(),profile:{...p,duration:n},days};}
function validate(raw){if(raw?.schema!=='LT-JOURNEY-1'||!raw.profile||![21,30,40].includes(raw.profile.duration)||!Array.isArray(raw.days)||raw.days.length!==raw.profile.duration)throw Error('Choose a valid LifeTogether 21-, 30-, or 40-day journey backup.');const clean=(s,max=20000)=>String(s??'').slice(0,max),p=raw.profile;const profile={title:clean(p.title,250),subtitle:clean(p.subtitle,600),prepared:clean(p.prepared,180),duration:p.duration,role:p.role==='Advisor'?'Advisor':'Pastor',focus:themes[p.focus]?p.focus:'formation',date:/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/.test(p.date||'')?p.date:'',notes:clean(p.notes,60000),sourceId:clean(p.sourceId,100),sourceTitle:clean(p.sourceTitle,250),sourceSubtitle:clean(p.sourceSubtitle,600),source:clean(p.source,2000)};if(!profile.title.trim())throw Error('Backup has no title.');return{schema:'LT-JOURNEY-1',created:clean(raw.created,100),profile,days:raw.days.map((d,i)=>{if(!d||typeof d!=='object')throw Error('Invalid daily entry.');return{number:i+1,...Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(fields).map(k=>[k,clean(d[k])])),reviewed:d.reviewed===true};})};}
function date(p,i){if(!/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/.test(p.date||''))return '';const d=new Date(p.date+'T12:00:00Z');if(!Number.isFinite(+d))return '';d.setUTCDate(d.getUTCDate()+i);return d.toISOString().slice(0,10);}
function sessions(j){const out=[];for(let i=0;i<j.days.length;i+=7){const ds=j.days.slice(i,i+7);out.push({number:out.length+1,title:ds[0].title,range:`Days ${i+1}–${i+ds.length}`,body:`Gather and check in: What has stayed with you from these readings?\nRead together: ${ds[0].scripture}. Notice its original setting and central teaching.\nDiscuss: Where did you find encouragement? What challenged you? What question would you like to explore?\nPractice: Each person may share one next step and the support they would welcome.\nPray and follow up: Agree on a time to reconnect. Participation and personal disclosure are voluntary.`});}return out;}
const status=j=>j.days.every(d=>d.reviewed)?'EDITOR REVIEW RECORDED — confirm source permissions before distribution':'EDITABLE DEVOTIONAL DRAFT — review before distribution';
function markdown(j){const p=j.profile;return`# ${p.title}\n\n${p.subtitle}\n\n${p.duration}-day ${p.role==='Advisor'?'journey':'campaign'} · ${p.prepared||'LifeTogether'}\n${status(j)}\n\nOriginal guided reflections; not a reproduction of The Purpose Driven Life. Source catalog titles do not certify a recovered manuscript.\n\n`+j.days.map(d=>`## Day ${d.number}: ${d.title}\n\n${date(p,d.number-1)}\n\n`+Object.entries(fields).filter(([k])=>k!=='title').map(([k,l])=>`### ${l}\n\n${d[k]}`).join('\n\n')).join('\n\n---\n\n')+'\n\n# Group or family conversations\n\n'+sessions(j).map(s=>`## Conversation ${s.number}: ${s.title}\n${s.range}\n\n${s.body}`).join('\n\n')+`\n\n# Source and edition notes\n\nSource ID: ${p.sourceId||'Custom title'}\nOriginal title: ${p.sourceTitle||p.title}\nOriginal subtitle: ${p.sourceSubtitle||'Not supplied'}\nSource: ${p.source||'Original title entered in Journey Studio'}\nDevotional text: original guided framework, editable by the preparer.\n\nPrepared source notes:\n${p.notes||'No source text supplied.'}`;}
const printCSS='@page{size:letter;margin:.65in}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{font:10.5pt/1.4 Georgia,serif;color:#152638;margin:0;background:#eee}main{max-width:8.5in;margin:auto;background:white}section{padding:.65in;break-before:page}section:first-child{break-before:auto}h1{font-size:34pt;line-height:1.12}h2{font-size:22pt;line-height:1.15}h3{font:700 9pt system-ui;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;margin:12px 0 5px;color:#795721;break-after:avoid}p{white-space:pre-wrap;margin:4px 0 8px;overflow-wrap:anywhere;orphans:3;widows:3}.meta{font:9pt/1.6 system-ui;color:#546174}.line{border-bottom:1px solid #bcb7ac;height:26px}.cover{padding-top:1.5in;min-height:9in}.gold{color:#997435}.toolbar{background:#0b1726;padding:15px;text-align:center;color:white;font:14px system-ui}.toolbar button{font:inherit;padding:10px 16px;cursor:pointer}.source{font-size:10pt}.toc{columns:2;font-size:10pt}a{color:inherit}@media print{body{background:white}main{max-width:none}section{padding:0}.toolbar{display:none}.cover{min-height:auto;height:8.6in}.day{break-after:page}a{text-decoration:none}}';
function html(j){const p=j.profile,E=escape;return'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>'+E(p.title)+' · Print edition</title><style>'+printCSS+'</style></head><body><div class="toolbar"><button onclick="window.print()">Print / Save as PDF</button><p>Choose Letter or A4, default scale, and turn off browser headers and footers.</p></div><main><section class="cover"><p class="meta gold">LIFETOGETHER · '+p.duration+' DAYS</p><h1>'+E(p.title)+'</h1><p>'+E(p.subtitle)+'</p><p>Prepared for '+E(p.prepared||'your journey')+'</p><p class="meta">'+E(status(j))+'</p><p class="meta">Read Scripture in your preferred translation. Reflect honestly. Choose a practical response. Return tomorrow.</p><p class="meta">An original guided devotional framework with editable teaching. Not a reproduction of The Purpose Driven Life or a claim that a source manuscript has been recovered.</p></section><section><h2>Your daily rhythm</h2><p>Begin with Scripture, consider the teaching, ponder one truth, practice it, reflect on three questions, and pray. Use the writing space for your next step. The closing conversations can be used by a small group, family, or advisor and client.</p><div class="toc">'+j.days.map(d=>'<p>'+d.number+'. '+E(d.title)+'</p>').join('')+'</div></section>'+j.days.map(d=>'<section class="day"><p class="meta">DAY '+d.number+' / '+p.duration+' · '+E(date(p,d.number-1))+' · '+(d.reviewed?'Editor reviewed':'Draft')+'</p><h2>'+E(d.title)+'</h2>'+Object.entries(fields).filter(([k])=>k!=='title').map(([k,l])=>'<h3>'+l+'</h3><p>'+E(d[k])+'</p>').join('')+'<div class="line"></div><div class="line"></div></section>').join('')+sessions(j).map(s=>'<section><p class="meta">CONVERSATION '+s.number+' · '+s.range+'</p><h2>'+E(s.title)+'</h2><p>'+E(s.body)+'</p><div class="line"></div><div class="line"></div><div class="line"></div></section>').join('')+'<section class="source"><h2>Source and edition notes</h2><p>Source ID: '+E(p.sourceId||'Custom title')+'\nOriginal title: '+E(p.sourceTitle||p.title)+'\nOriginal subtitle: '+E(p.sourceSubtitle||'Not supplied')+'\nSource: '+E(p.source||'Entered by preparer')+'</p><p>Daily reflections are an original editorial framework. They are not quotations from the catalog source, a complete adaptation of your pasted notes, or an approved manuscript. Confirm scriptural fit, permissions, and audience suitability before distribution.</p><h3>Prepared source notes</h3><p>'+E(p.notes||'No source text supplied.')+'</p></section></main></body></html>';}
return{escape,norm,fields,beats,themes,restricted,records,build,validate,sessions,markdown,html};})();
if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=JourneyEngine;
